WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.659 --> 00:00:03.600
Movie bloopers are a lot of fun, plain and
simple.

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:05.630
Film fans love to watch actors flub.

00:00:05.630 --> 00:00:09.680
And considering the way they make themselves
and their co-stars laugh when it happens,

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:11.120
actors clearly love bloopers too.

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:14.430
Filmmakers, on the other hand, may not love
them quite as much.

00:00:14.430 --> 00:00:18.519
Every time an actor makes a mistake, it delays
the shoot just a little, and time is money.

00:00:18.519 --> 00:00:22.640
And every so often, they're forced to deal
with a truly world-class mess-up — a mistake

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:27.150
that costs the production big money due to
property damage and personal injury.

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:28.150
Seven

00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:30.570
Brad Pitt suffered a nasty injury on the set
of Seven.

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:34.680
During one scene that called for him to chase
his suspect in the rain, he slipped and smashed

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:37.520
his hand into a windshield, severing a tendon.

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:41.520
Director David Fincher mentions on the film's
DVD commentary that Pitt showed him his wound,

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:44.200
which was so severe he could see the white
of the bone.

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:48.289
Making the best of a bad situation, Fincher
managed to incorporate Pitt's injury into

00:00:48.289 --> 00:00:52.430
the story of Seven by having the script rewritten
to have Pitt's character suffer an injury

00:00:52.430 --> 00:00:53.430
in the line of duty.

00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:56.050
It added time and expense to the production
schedule.

00:00:56.050 --> 00:00:59.629
But it was probably a lot cheaper to explain
why one of the film's main characters was

00:00:59.629 --> 00:01:03.700
suddenly wearing a cast than simply delaying
shooting long enough for Pitt to recover.

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:08.190
Still, it didn't solve the problem completely:
like most movies, Seven was shot out of sequence,

00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:12.470
so any earlier scene filmed after Pitt's injury
required the actor to hold his bad hand in

00:01:12.470 --> 00:01:15.190
his pocket or behind his back.

00:01:15.190 --> 00:01:16.480
Skyfall

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:20.620
According to writer and filmmaker Charlie
Lyne, the 2012 James Bond adventure Skyfall

00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:25.100
had to deal with some unplanned expenses because
star Daniel Craig wore a pair of leather gloves

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:26.100
at the wrong moment.

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:29.850
As Lyne told the story via Twitter, which
he heard from someone who worked on the film,

00:01:29.850 --> 00:01:33.600
Craig asked director Sam Mendes if he could
wear a pair of leather gloves in a particular

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:34.600
scene.

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:37.780
Mendes agreed, and the cast and crew got down
to work filming a sequence in which a bad

00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:41.920
guy gets Bond's gun away from him, only to
find he can't fire it because it has a fingerprint

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:43.740
scanner — it will only work for Bond.

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:46.250
"There's a microdermal sensor in the grip.

00:01:46.250 --> 00:01:49.710
It's been coded to your palmprint so only
you can fire it."

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:52.900
Months later in the editing bay, a production
assistant noticed a problem:

00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:57.220
"Then how is Bond ever going to fire it wearing
gloves?

00:01:57.220 --> 00:02:00.290
And so silence falls across the room."

00:02:00.290 --> 00:02:03.540
After considering the costs of reshooting
the scene, the filmmakers decided it would

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:07.240
be cheaper — although still quite expensive
— to digitally remove the gloves from Craig's

00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:08.800
hands for the entire sequence.

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:15.190
"If you watch the scene now, honest to god,
you can see that Bond has these ridiculously

00:02:15.190 --> 00:02:16.750
pudgy hands.

00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:19.140
He has these, like, Mr. Blobby hands.

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:23.360
Because in every single frame, he's wearing
these thick leather gloves that have been

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:27.980
painted over with Craig-toned hands.

00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:29.390
The Hateful Eight

00:02:29.390 --> 00:02:33.480
To play the crafty outlaw Daisy Domergue in
Quentin Tarantino's The Hateful Eight, Jennifer

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:35.530
Jason Leigh had to learn how to play the guitar.

00:02:35.530 --> 00:02:39.480
The lucky actress got to learn on the same
one she'd strum in the film: a vintage Martin

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:43.340
guitar dating to the 19th century, loaned
to the movie by the Martin Guitar Museum.

00:02:43.340 --> 00:02:47.420
Leigh's big guitar-playing moment on screen
was all planned out: while she was playing,

00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:49.170
co-star Kurt Russell was supposed to grab
it.

00:02:49.170 --> 00:02:52.420
The scene would cut and the Martin would be
traded out for a cheaper prop guitar, which

00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:54.960
Russell would smash when the cameras started
to roll again.

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:59.010
But that isn't what happened — instead,
Russell grabbed the real Martin from Leigh

00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:00.380
and smashed it.

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:02.400
"Gimme that guitar."

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:06.720
"Music time's over!"

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:13.620
Leigh later told Billboard, "Kurt felt terrible;
he had no idea."

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:14.800
Justice League

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:18.810
Sometimes it's not the actor who goofs up,
it's the studio responsible for the movie

00:03:18.810 --> 00:03:21.650
— and then they have to fork out the cash
to make things right.

00:03:21.650 --> 00:03:25.930
Warner Bros. had to reassemble the cast of
Justice League in the summer of 2017 for some

00:03:25.930 --> 00:03:27.120
crucial reshoots.

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:30.990
Henry Cavill, who plays Superman, was among
those summoned, but there was a problem: He'd

00:03:30.990 --> 00:03:35.660
grown a mustache for his next role, in the
sixth Mission: Impossible movie, and was contractually

00:03:35.660 --> 00:03:38.780
obliged to keep it until M:I 6 was finished
filming.

00:03:38.780 --> 00:03:43.100
The facial hair scheduling snafu left Justice
League's visual effects technicians with the

00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:46.000
job of digitally removing Superman's mustache.

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:50.110
Effects expert Dave Fleet told Business Insider
it could cost millions: "We would need to

00:03:50.110 --> 00:03:54.500
build a 3D model of the face and then shade
the surface of the skin to look realistic.

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:58.770
We would then need to track the 3D model to
the movement of his head and potentially re-animate

00:03:58.770 --> 00:03:59.770
his mouth."

00:03:59.770 --> 00:04:02.120
"That mustache has got to go!

00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:04.760
It makes you look like a total geek…

00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:06.740
It makes you look like a total geek…"

00:04:07.590 --> 00:04:08.580
The Avengers

00:04:08.590 --> 00:04:12.400
Marvel's superhero team isn't the only Avengers
in pop culture.

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:16.410
There's also The Avengers, a 1960s British
spy TV series.

00:04:16.410 --> 00:04:21.780
The 1998 film adaptation starring Ralph Fiennesand
Uma Thurman was a critical and commercial

00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:24.960
bomb, but the movie made headlines for another
type of detonation.

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:28.620
While the crew filmed an explosion for the
movie, a few rogue sparks drifted away and

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:32.500
hit the roof of the studio, starting a fire
that was definitely not part of the plan.

00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:37.950
A set worth a reported $1.6 million was destroyed,
along with the hopes of any sequel.

00:04:37.950 --> 00:04:41.090
"Never trust the weather, Sir August."

00:04:41.090 --> 00:04:44.130
"Rain or shine, all is mine!"

00:04:44.130 --> 00:04:45.180
Thanks for watching!

00:04:45.180 --> 00:04:47.620
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:49.940
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

